    "result_text": "The full test suite consists of just this one test class (2 tests), and both pass with BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented locality derivation from city for the PetClinic REST app so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns a `locality` field.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerLocality.java`** (new) \u2014 A small static helper holding the fixed table (`Sydney\u2192NSW`, `Melbourne\u2192VIC`, `Brisbane\u2192QLD`) with `forCity(...)` returning the canonical region, or `\"UNKNOWN\"` for any other (or null) city.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Mapping` for `locality` that calls `OwnerLocality.forCity(owner.getCity())`.\n\n**Key gotcha I hit and fixed:** My first attempt put the derivation as a `default String localityForCity(String)` method directly on the MapStruct `@Mapper` interface. MapStruct auto-selected that `String\u2192String` method as an *implicit conversion for every String property*, so it wrapped `telephone`, `city`, etc. with it \u2014 corrupting the telephone (turning `0400000004` into `\"UNKNOWN\"`, which then failed E.164 normalization with a 400). Moving the logic into a separate static helper class referenced only via the `expression` keeps it out of MapStruct's method-selection and leaves all other fields untouched.\n\nBoth tests (`coreDerivesRegionForKnownCity` \u2192 `NSW` for Sydney, `functionalityUnknownCityIsUnknown` \u2192 `UNKNOWN` for an unlisted city) pass, and the suite is green.",
